# Flutter and Dart Development Guidelines

## Clean Architecture Rule
- Every feature must follow clean architecture with proper layer separation
- Maintain clear boundaries between presentation, domain, and data layers
- Use dependency injection with GetIt
  - Use singleton for services and repositories
  - Use factory for use cases
  - Use lazy singleton for controllers
- Follow repository pattern for data access
- Keep dependencies flowing inward
- Organize code in feature-based modules

## Dart General Guidelines

### Basic Principles
- Use English for all code and documentation
- Always declare types explicitly (variables, function parameters, and return values)
- Avoid using `dynamic` or `any`
- Create necessary types instead of using primitives excessively
- Don't leave blank lines within a function
- One export per file
- Follow SOLID principles
- Prefer composition over inheritance

### Nomenclature
- Use PascalCase for classes
- Use camelCase for variables, functions, and methods
- Use underscores_case for file and directory names
- Use UPPERCASE for environment variables
- Avoid magic numbers and define constants
- Start each function with a verb
- Use verbs for boolean variables (isLoading, hasError, canDelete)
- Use complete words instead of abbreviations except for:
  - Standard terms (API, URL)
  - Loop variables (i, j)
  - Error handling (err)
  - Context variables (ctx)
  - HTTP parameters (req, res)

### Functions and Methods
- Keep functions short (< 20 instructions) with single responsibility
- Use early returns and avoid deep nesting
- Use higher-order functions when appropriate
- Use arrow syntax for simple functions
- Use named functions for complex logic
- Use default parameter values
- Follow RO-RO (Receive Object, Return Object) pattern
- Maintain single level of abstraction
- Declare input/output types explicitly

### Classes
- Follow SOLID principles
- Keep classes small (< 200 instructions)
- Limit to < 10 public methods
- Limit to < 10 properties
- Prefer immutability
- Use `const` constructors where possible
- Declare interfaces for contracts

## Flutter-Specific Guidelines

### Widget Structure
- Break down complex widgets into smaller, reusable components
- Avoid deep widget nesting
- Utilize const constructors to reduce rebuilds
- Keep widget tree flat for better performance
- Follow proper widget lifecycle methods
- Implement proper error boundaries

### State Management
- Use GetX for state management
- Implement reactive programming with .obs properties
- Avoid unnecessary widget rebuilds
- Controllers should take methods as input and update UI state
- Use proper state management patterns

### Navigation and Routing
- Use AutoRoute for navigation
- Use extras to pass data between pages
- Implement proper routing structure

### Performance
- Cache network requests appropriately
- Profile performance regularly
- Implement proper list view optimization
- Use proper build methods optimization
- Implement proper memory management
- Use proper platform channels when needed

### Code Organization
- Follow the standard project structure:
  ```
  lib/
    core/
      constants/
      theme/
      utils/
      widgets/
    features/
      feature_name/
        data/
          datasources/
          models/
          repositories/
        domain/
          entities/
          repositories/
          usecases/
        presentation/
          bloc/
          pages/
          widgets/
    l10n/
    main.dart
  ```

### Testing
- Write unit tests for business logic
- Implement widget tests for UI components
- Use integration tests for API modules
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Use clear test naming conventions:
  - inputX
  - mockX
  - actualX
  - expectedX
- Write acceptance tests for modules
- Follow Given-When-Then convention

### Error Handling
- Use exceptions for unexpected errors
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Add context when catching exceptions
- Use global error handler when appropriate

### Additional Best Practices
- Use ThemeData for consistent styling
- Implement proper localization with AppLocalizations
- Use extensions for reusable code
- Follow Material 3 design principles
- Implement proper form validation
- Use proper asset management
- Follow platform-specific design guidelines

## Additional Flutter Guidelines

### Data Management
- Don't abuse primitive types and encapsulate data in composite types
- Avoid data validations in functions and use classes with internal validation
- Prefer immutability for data
- Use readonly for data that doesn't change
- Use as const for literals that don't change

### Exception Handling Specifics
- Use exceptions to handle errors you don't expect
- If you catch an exception, it should be to:
  - Fix an expected problem
  - Add context
  - Otherwise, use a global handler

### Module Organization
- See modules if you need to organize code into modules
- See controllers if you need to organize code into controllers
- See services if you need to organize code into services
- See repositories if you need to organize code into repositories
- See entities if you need to organize code into entities

### Caching Strategy
- See cache if you need to cache data
- Controller always takes methods as input and updates the UI state that effects the UI

### Widget Tree Optimization
- When a widget tree becomes too deep, it can lead to longer build times and increased memory usage
- Flutter needs to traverse the entire tree to render the UI, so a flatter structure improves efficiency
- A flatter widget structure makes it easier to understand and modify the code
- Reusable components facilitate better code organization
- Deeply nested widgets can make state management more challenging
- By keeping the tree shallow, it becomes easier to manage state and pass data between widgets

### Testing Specifics
- Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert convention for tests
- Name test variables clearly following conventions:
  - inputX for input values
  - mockX for mock objects
  - actualX for actual results
  - expectedX for expected results
- Write unit tests for each public function
- Use test doubles to simulate dependencies
  - Except for third-party dependencies that are not expensive to execute
- Write acceptance tests for each module
- Follow the Given-When-Then convention for acceptance tests

These rules are mandatory and will be automatically enforced. While maintaining flexibility to adapt to existing project structures, these guidelines ensure code quality, maintainability, and performance.